WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.640
On his second China trip this year, Trump will not press Xi on the Huang spy finding. This is Omni View North America, episode 49. I'm Mira, the Nerra Network's AI host. From there, a Canadian border agency is moving officers off armed posts.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.580
Vladimir Putin. NPR is the newsroom that puts NVIDIA fallout over that

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:38.500
itinerary. The New York Times is the one that reports the intelligence findings

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:44.040
themselves. A Chinese spy targeted NVIDIA's Jensen Huang. NPR also ties the

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:48.080
timing to politics at home, saying the president and his party faced widespread

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:52.340
backlash from voters in the recent midterm elections. Those two accounts are what

00:00:52.340 --> 00:00:57.066
sit in front of the she meeting, with Putin also on the schedule. In Canada,

00:00:57.066 --> 00:01:02.206
The Canada Border Services Agency has told 63 employees they must move into new jobs

00:01:02.206 --> 00:01:02.906
that pay less.

00:01:03.566 --> 00:01:08.106
CBC reports the reason as medical conditions that keep them from carrying a sidearm and

00:01:08.106 --> 00:01:12.126
describes the shift as a demotion of officers who have permanently lost that ability.

00:01:12.666 --> 00:01:16.806
The notices are the agency's own decision on how to accommodate those 63 people.

00:01:17.406 --> 00:01:22.926
San Diego is where a SNAP office is now doubling as a food pantry, NPR reports, while millions

00:01:22.926 --> 00:01:26.226
lose food aid nationwide under cuts and changes to the program.

00:01:26.226 --> 00:01:31.186
The same account says those cuts have been hard on government workers who take pride in helping others.

00:01:31.666 --> 00:01:35.406
In that city, the workers said they could not sit back while neighbors went hungry.

00:01:35.966 --> 00:01:39.386
The extra role is their local answer to a national cut in food assistance.

00:01:40.609 --> 00:01:46.369
Flight disruptions are expected to run into Monday, NPR reports, after Friday's nationwide ground stop.

00:01:46.829 --> 00:01:52.909
Congress has given the Department of Transportation $12.5 billion to upgrade the aging air traffic control system,

00:01:52.909 --> 00:01:54.649
them, and President Trump wants more.

00:01:55.169 --> 00:01:59.329
The New York Times says operations are returning to normal after air traffic was shut down

00:01:59.329 --> 00:02:03.429
for more than four hours on Friday, when a maintenance upgrade crippled communications.

00:02:04.189 --> 00:02:08.449
NPR's morning news brief adds that thousands of travelers are still waiting to reschedule.

00:02:08.869 --> 00:02:13.269
The money already voted, and the further some the president wants, sit next to a system

00:02:13.269 --> 00:02:15.669
still clearing that shutdown into the start of the week.

00:02:16.648 --> 00:02:21.048
Halifax is where the North Atlantic right-well consortium put out a new estimate on Monday,

00:02:21.048 --> 00:02:23.628
and the globe and mail is the paper carrying it.

00:02:24.068 --> 00:02:30.668
About 382 animals at the end of 2025, that is seven fewer than a year earlier, 20% under

00:02:30.668 --> 00:02:35.328
the 2011 peak of 483, and it ends four years of fragile growth.

00:02:35.648 --> 00:02:39.608
After that peak, the species lost about a quarter of its numbers in less than a decade.

00:02:40.168 --> 00:02:44.748
Heather Pettis of the New England Aquarium, who chairs the consortium, said this population

00:02:44.748 --> 00:02:46.568
cannot afford another slide like that.

00:02:47.068 --> 00:02:50.008
Fishing gear and tanglements and ship strikes remain the main threats.

00:02:50.008 --> 00:02:54.168
The whales move between Canadian and U.S. waters to feed and to give birth.

00:02:54.648 --> 00:02:58.968
A hotter ocean has pushed them into new feeding grounds, and ocean noise adds pressure.

00:02:59.588 --> 00:03:03.828
Scientists say both countries have to act if the animals are to recover, and Pettis says

00:03:03.828 --> 00:03:05.628
the two approaches are now pulling apart.

00:03:06.028 --> 00:03:10.588
In the United States, she said, the movement is to deregulate and to backslide on protections.

00:03:11.128 --> 00:03:15.308
Congress has barred new entanglement rules for U.S. lobster and crab fisheries through

00:03:15.308 --> 00:03:16.088
2028.

00:03:16.088 --> 00:03:21.368
The House has passed a bill, still waiting on the Senate, to stretch that pause to 2035.

00:03:22.048 --> 00:03:25.868
Pettis said the extension could stall the next round of rules, and that a delay is the

00:03:25.868 --> 00:03:26.728
last thing needed.

00:03:27.308 --> 00:03:32.368
NOAA is looking at deregulatory action on the 2008 rule that makes large ships slow down

00:03:32.368 --> 00:03:36.848
in seasonal right-wheel zones along the U.S. East Coast, including the idea of trading

00:03:36.848 --> 00:03:41.548
speed limits for acoustic or other gear that spots whales and worn ships in real time.

00:03:41.548 --> 00:03:47.548
John Chu, a NOAA Fisheries spokesperson, said that 2008 rule stays in force while public

00:03:47.548 --> 00:03:48.488
comments are reviewed.

00:03:49.068 --> 00:03:52.768
Pettis said the new tools are promising and not ready to stand in for the slowdown.

00:03:53.268 --> 00:03:55.208
She called them an AND, not an OR.

00:03:55.708 --> 00:04:00.808
A separate federal rule, in effects in September 14, narrows what counts as harm under the

00:04:00.808 --> 00:04:02.028
Endangered Species Act.

00:04:02.368 --> 00:04:06.808
Because these deaths are incidental, she called that cause for alarm, even though the Marine

00:04:06.808 --> 00:04:08.928
Mammal Protection Act still covers the whales.

00:04:08.928 --> 00:04:14.428
Chew said the harm change is aimed at unnecessary or duplicative permits for projects and industries,

00:04:14.808 --> 00:04:18.548
not at stripping protections, and that cutting risk has to be a shared job.

00:04:19.148 --> 00:04:20.968
Pettis called Canada much more nimble.

00:04:21.468 --> 00:04:25.588
Sean Brilliant of the Canadian Wildlife Federation said that if Washington pulls back,

00:04:26.028 --> 00:04:27.848
Ottawa has to hold the line or step up.

00:04:28.268 --> 00:04:32.028
Canada first closed fisheries and limited ship speeds after a wave of deaths

00:04:32.028 --> 00:04:34.128
in the Gulf of St. Lawrence in 2017.

00:04:34.788 --> 00:04:37.488
Brilliant said that, at best, ended the nose dive,

00:04:37.488 --> 00:04:39.968
and that the species is still on a knife's edge.

00:04:40.428 --> 00:04:43.628
Two of the three whales found tangled in gear this year were in that gulf.

00:04:43.968 --> 00:04:48.728
The latest on October 21st, according to Lindsay Jones, who manages the catalog at the

00:04:48.728 --> 00:04:49.708
New England Aquarium.

00:04:50.128 --> 00:04:55.208
In February, fisheries and oceans Canada launched a whale-safe fishing gear strategy, meant

00:04:55.208 --> 00:04:59.968
to keep grounds open when whales are present, by letting crews use on-demand or ropeless

00:04:59.968 --> 00:05:03.308
gear that holds buoy lines on the seabed until the trap comes up.

00:05:03.308 --> 00:05:07.908
Alexis Van Behmel of the department said Canadian harvesters in industry have led

00:05:07.908 --> 00:05:08.488
the testing.

00:05:09.068 --> 00:05:14.028
After closures near North Lake, Prince Edward Island and Cheta Camp, Nova Scotia, the

00:05:14.028 --> 00:05:19.088
Federation's Cannes Fish Lending Program trained about 85 harvesters and decans, nearly

00:05:19.088 --> 00:05:24.168
as many as in every year since 2022 combined, and lobster was landed commercially out of

00:05:24.168 --> 00:05:26.408
a Canadian right-wheel closure for the first time.

00:05:26.968 --> 00:05:28.448
Old rope is a different problem.

00:05:28.448 --> 00:05:34.568
A four-year-old male in the catalog, called Division, number 5217, was found dead off North

00:05:34.568 --> 00:05:39.328
Carolina in January, wrapped in line from a snow crab area in the southern Gulf that had

00:05:39.328 --> 00:05:40.728
not been fished in five years.

00:05:41.328 --> 00:05:45.268
Marina Petrovic of the department said gear-marking rules let officials trace it.

00:05:45.568 --> 00:05:51.648
Since 2019, the Ghost Gear program has pulled up more than 2,620 tons of lost gear and more

00:05:51.648 --> 00:05:53.588
than 1,040 kilometers of rope.

00:05:54.148 --> 00:05:57.148
A national plan on that lost gear is expected next spring.

00:05:57.148 --> 00:06:03.208
The 2025-2026 season also produced 24 calves, the fourth highest on record.

